      "explanations": {
        "w044": "The chalice is the object of an understood “took.” The same action was stated explicitly with the bread: “accépit panem.”",
        "w069": "The chalice is the object of “bibétis,” which follows it: “you drink the chalice.” The Latin places the object before the verb.",
        "w086": "“Reus” takes the genitives “córporis et sánguinis”: the guilt concerns an offense against the Lord’s Body and Blood. It does not mean that the Body and Blood are guilty. “Indígne” describes the unworthy manner of receiving.",
        "w112": "Eating and drinking “judgment on himself” expresses the consequence of receiving unworthily. “Iudícium” here denotes adverse judgment. The word alone does not specify eternal damnation."
      }
    }
  },
